---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";
---
<Layout
  title="How to Wear Hanfu: Complete Step-by-Step Guide"
  description="Learn how to put on Hanfu correctly: inner layer, main robe, belt, accessories, hair, and shoes. Includes the golden rule of collar direction and dynasty-specific layering."
  canonicalUrl="https://www.dresses-hanfu.com/guides/how-to-wear/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Guides", url: "/guides" }, { name: "How to Wear Hanfu", url: "/guides/how-to-wear" }]}
  articleSchema={{
    datePublished: "2026-08-01",
    dateModified: "2026-08-08",
    author: { "@type": "Person", "name": "Hanfu Dress Guide Editorial Team" }
  }}
>
  <SiteHeader active="/guides/how-to-wear" />
  <main>
    <article>
      <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-bottom:1rem;"><a href="/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Guides</a> &rsaquo; How to Wear Hanfu</p>
          <h1>How to Wear Hanfu: A Complete Step-by-Step Guide</h1>
          <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:1.5rem;line-height:1.55;">
            Dressing in Hanfu is simpler than it looks — once you know the order and the one rule that can't be broken: the collar always crosses right over left. Here is the full sequence, from inner layer to final accessory.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <div class="step-list" style="max-width:100%;">
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">1</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h2>Start with the Inner Layer (Zhongyi)</h2>
                <p>Wear a white or light-colored cotton, silk, or blend inner garment against the skin. It provides comfort and a base for layering, and it protects the outer silks from sweat. Cross the collar right over left and secure the inner tie before continuing.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">2</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h2>Add the Main Robe (Aoqun or Ruqun)</h2>
                <p>This is the centerpiece. For women, the classic set is an aoqun (jacket plus skirt) or ruqun (cross-collar top plus wrap skirt). Wrap the skirt at your natural waist or, for Tang style, at the chest. The top goes on next, collar crossing right over left.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">3</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h2>Secure with Belt and Sash</h2>
                <p>Tie a fabric sash or decorative belt at the waist. This is what creates the flowing Hanfu silhouette and holds all layers in place. Silk ribbons and leather belts both work; match the material to the dynasty style you're wearing.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">4</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h2>Layer Outer Pieces (Optional)</h2>
                <p>For extra depth and weather, add a beizi jacket (Song style), a daxiu shan open robe (Tang style), or a long Ming dynasty outer robe. Keep the layering intentional: more layers signals formality, so choose based on the occasion.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">5</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h2>Add Accessories</h2>
                <p>Hairpins (zan), jade pendants, decorative fans, sachets, and tassels complete the look. Accessories signal season, status, and occasion — and they're where personal style shows most. Start with one or two pieces rather than everything at once.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">6</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h2>Finish with Hair and Footwear</h2>
                <p>Style hair in a traditional updo, half-up, or braid appropriate to the dynasty (see <a href="/guides/hairstyles/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Hanfu Hairstyles</a>). Finish with embroidered cloth shoes or boots. Formal occasions call for a hair crown or headdress.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">The Golden Rule: Right Over Left</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Repeating because it's that important: Hanfu collars always cross right over left. The reverse — left over right — is the traditional way the deceased are dressed, and wearing it in the wrong direction in a cultural context is a serious faux pas. Check your collar before heading out, and again in photos.
          </p>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">How to Wear Hanfu by Dynasty</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Each dynasty layered the basics differently. Here's the cheat sheet:
          </p>
          <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
            <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
              <thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Core Pieces</th><th>Key Difference</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Han</strong></td><td>Shenyi (one-piece wrap robe)</td><td>Skirt and robe joined into a single garment</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Tang</strong></td><td>Ruqun + qixiong skirt + daxiu shan</td><td>Skirt tied at the chest; dramatic wide sleeves</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Song</strong></td><td>Ruqun + beizi jacket</td><td>Slim fit, muted colors, straight narrow sleeves</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Ming</strong></td><td>Aoqun + mamianqun + standing collar</td><td>Structured, tailored-looking, often embroidered</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Modern</strong></td><td>Simplified cross-collar top + pants or skirt</td><td>Machine-washable, practical, everyday</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:0.75rem;">Style deep-dives: <a href="/styles/tang/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Tang</a> · <a href="/styles/song/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Song</a> · <a href="/styles/ming/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Ming</a> · <a href="/styles/modern/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Modern</a></p>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
          <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
            <li><strong>Wrong collar direction</strong> — the number one mistake. Right over left, always.</li>
            <li><strong>Sash too loose</strong> — a loose sash makes layers slide and looks sloppy. Tie it snugly at the waist or ribcage.</li>
            <li><strong>Mixed dynasty pieces</strong> — pairing a Tang skirt with a Ming collar can look off. Stick to one dynasty per outfit until you know the system.</li>
            <li><strong>Wearing modern underwear lines</strong> — loose silhouettes show lines easily; choose seamless or smooth undergarments.</li>
            <li><strong>Ignoring shoes</strong> — modern sneakers can work with casual Hanfu, but embroidered cloth shoes complete the look for formal wear.</li>
          </ul>

          <div style="margin-top:3rem;padding:2rem;background:var(--color-accent-light);border-radius:var(--radius-md);">
            <p style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;font-family:var(--font-display);">Next Steps</p>
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.75rem;margin-top:1rem;">
              <a href="/guides/hairstyles/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu Hairstyles</a>
              <a href="/guides/etiquette/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu Etiquette</a>
              <a href="/guides/sizing/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu Size Guide</a>
              <a href="/buying/beginner/" class="btn btn-outline">Beginner's Buying Guide</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>